\chapter{Reading the roadmap and extending the system}
\label{ch:120}
\index{Reading the roadmap and extending the system}

Roadmap and design documents describe intended breadth. Current implementation, target checks and executable fixtures establish what can be used. Full async/await lowering, general resource-bearing foreign calls, and wider checked backend representations require more than source-level syntax. Native C11 can execute features that standard MIR cannot represent. The support profile you choose should follow the application and its evidence.

\section{Audit a feature end to end}
Choose an operation such as passing framed data to a kernel-backed service. Name the source construct or facade, the ownership and error model, the checked transformation, the provider and artifact, and the tests that execute both outcome paths. If a step lacks a concrete admission model, identify the representation or proof it needs before making the application depend on it. The same audit works for a new optimizer, runtime SDK callback, or library feature. Revisit the capability matrix and the verification record at the revision you intend to ship.

\section{Audit the claim that a bounds check disappeared}
Take the MEMGraph regression that constructs a two-element array
containing ten and twenty and returns the element at index one.
The ordinary direct C11 emission contains a bounds helper call. In
the enabled fixed-array profile the checked base supplies extent two,
the index is the exact i64 literal one, and the predicate $0\leq
1<2$ is true. The emitter can omit that call. The test looks for
its absence, checks that one decision was applied, and then emits the
ordinary version again from the same unit. It must match the original
reference output. This last step distinguishes a private lowering
decision from a mutation of the checked program seen by later clients.

Try index two. It is out of bounds as an element index, even though
the slice starting and ending at two is a legal empty slice. The
two predicates differ at their upper boundary. A dynamic index must
retain its runtime check when the proof has no exact value. Neither
the optimized nor the reference path gets to skip evaluating the
base expression. Counting fewer helper calls without checking these
neighboring cases would not establish preservation of traps or effects.

Finally ask what happens if the checked representation itself is
inconsistent. The regression changes the index operand's type and
expects verifier rejection with the existing report untouched. A
matching rule name is not permission to trust malformed input.
The defensible roadmap statement is accordingly narrow: verified
fixed-array operations can omit a proved redundant check in the
supported lowering. It does not imply an optimizer for arbitrary
loan lifetimes, foreign pointers or standard MIR aggregate layouts.

